{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2830 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c3652c623479a7dc9cc8384fe534d03ddff5c0b9237374d1e93b607d47fb49f2" :edb/db-before-t 2829 :edb/db-after-t 2830 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536142 50 #inst "2026-10-09T16:46:44.699Z" 13194139536142 true] [17592186096354 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/f02616ea8f44865a727bec3affe596503dbadacafb113acad6e5f755218b4ff5.png)\n\nFor the function $y = -2x^3+2$ shown above, write down an expression for the area $\\mathcal{A}$ as a definite integral." 13194139536142 true] [17592186096354 1100 "For the function $y = -2x^3+2$ shown above, write down an expression for the area $\\mathcal{A}$ as a definite integral.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1086/Source/Images/0cc7c2b91797e6c24577ac66cfd8e215.png)" 13194139536142 false] [17592186096354 1145 "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x^3+2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1$.\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x^3+2\n$$\n\nbetween the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$" 13194139536142 false] [17592186096354 1145 "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$" 13194139536142 true] [17592186096355 1145 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nthe function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4-x\n$$\n\nthe lower bound is $a=1$, and the upper bound is $b=3$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{3}(4 - x)dx &= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)f(x_{k})Δx \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[f(1 + k \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[f(1 + \\frac{2k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[(4 - (1 + \\frac{2k}{n})) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[(3 - \\frac{2k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536142 false] [17592186096355 1145 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = 4-x,$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(4-(1+\\frac{2k}{n}))\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(3-\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536142 true] [17592186096356 1100 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b$, given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right]$." 13194139536142 false] [17592186096356 1100 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].$" 13194139536142 true] [17592186096356 1145 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3$.\n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(x_k) = x_{k}^2\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^2\n$$\n\nand $b = 8$." 13194139536142 false] [17592186096356 1145 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}$ and $f(x_k) = x_{k}^2.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)= x^2$ and $b = 8.$" 13194139536142 true] [17592186096357 1145 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nthe function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nthe lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{2}x^{2}dx &= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)f(x_{k})Δx \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)[f(1 + k \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)[f(1 + \\frac{k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)[(1 + \\frac{k}{n})^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536142 false] [17592186096357 1145 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x =  \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = x^2,$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{2}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536142 true] [17592186096551 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385781 13194139536142 true] [17592186096551 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385788 13194139536142 true] [17592186096551 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385795 13194139536142 true] [17592186096552 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385742 13194139536142 true] [17592186096552 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385802 13194139536142 true] [17592186096552 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385826 13194139536142 true] [17592186096553 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385755 13194139536142 true] [17592186096553 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385762 13194139536142 true] [17592186096553 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385807 13194139536142 true] [17592186096554 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385819 13194139536142 true] [17592186096554 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385839 13194139536142 true] [17592186097129 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/04e636bd831dbfd45ebed1bc8928c4b68d479a1147b98474c667b2aee7a5efc1.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139536142 true] [17592186097129 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1086/Source/Images/q-30265.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x) =-x^{3} + 2x + 1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139536142 false] [17592186097129 1145 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x+1$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139536142 true] [17592186097130 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536142 true] [17592186097130 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/bb9db2eacfc01d00ca8a3436f9cd416afda7b400ae2aa2718d4ce049cd36a381.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139536142 true] [17592186097130 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1086/Source/Images/q-30273.png)\n\nFor the function $y = f(x) = x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139536142 false] [17592186097130 1145 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3$ between the lower limit $x=-1$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139536142 true] [17592186257007 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274289 13194139536142 false] [17592186257007 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385749 13194139536142 true] [17592186257007 1145 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4i}{n}-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536142 false] [17592186257007 1145 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4i}{n}-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536142 true] [17592186257016 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274292 13194139536142 false] [17592186257016 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385769 13194139536142 true] [17592186257022 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274294 13194139536142 false] [17592186257022 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385775 13194139536142 true] [17592186260600 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274759 13194139536142 false] [17592186260600 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385813 13194139536142 true] [17592186260600 1145 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$